# MAKATA.ai — Accessibility, Performance & Responsive Engineering Specification

**Artifact 19**

## 1. Purpose

This specification defines the engineering requirements for making the redesigned MAKATA.ai:

- fast on real devices
- excellent on mobile
- responsive across screen sizes
- accessible to keyboard and assistive-technology users
- resilient on slower networks and lower-powered devices
- efficient when loading interactive AI demonstrations
- visually stable during loading
- respectful of reduced-motion preferences
- measurable through both synthetic and real-world performance testing

The goal is not to make MAKATA.ai score perfectly on a benchmark.

The goal is to make the site **feel fast, remain usable, and expose technical work without the website itself becoming the bottleneck.**

### Core principle

> **Performance is part of the engineering experience.**

A visitor should be able to encounter the work before the website asks the browser to download everything required to explain it.

---

# 2. Performance Philosophy

MAKATA.ai has an unusual performance requirement.

It is simultaneously:

1. a professional portfolio
2. a technical documentation system
3. an interactive laboratory
4. a showcase for AI systems that may themselves be computationally expensive

The website therefore must distinguish between:

**Website performance**

and

**Demonstration/system performance.**

A 10-second AI inference should not make the entire webpage feel like a 10-second webpage.

Likewise, an architecture visualization should not require a large JavaScript bundle before the visitor can read the project description.

### Performance hierarchy

Prioritize:

1. Initial content
2. Primary navigation
3. Featured project
4. Primary CTA
5. Demonstration entry point
6. Supporting technical content
7. Secondary interaction
8. Advanced visualization
9. Decorative effects

Do not sacrifice the first six to improve the last three.

---

# 3. Performance Targets

These are **engineering targets**, not claims about guaranteed production measurements.

The actual results must be measured after implementation.

## 3.1 Core Web Vitals

Target strong real-world performance where practical:

| Metric |  Target | Interpretation                       |
| ------ | ------: | ------------------------------------ |
| LCP    |  ≤ 2.5s | Main content becomes visible quickly |
| INP    | ≤ 200ms | Interactions respond quickly         |
| CLS    |   ≤ 0.1 | Layout remains visually stable       |

These should be evaluated using both controlled testing and real-user measurements where sufficient traffic exists.

### Important distinction

A Lighthouse score is not equivalent to real-world performance.

A synthetic test can answer:

> “How does this page behave under this test configuration?”

It cannot conclusively answer:

> “How does MAKATA.ai perform on every visitor's phone, browser, network, and location?”

Therefore:

**Measured benchmark ≠ universal performance claim.**

---

# 4. Additional Diagnostic Metrics

Track additional metrics where useful:

- TTFB
- initial HTML response time
- JavaScript execution time
- total transferred bytes
- image bytes
- font bytes
- JavaScript bundle size
- time to interactive readiness where applicable
- demo initialization time
- demo execution time
- API latency
- AI inference time
- error rate

TTFB should be treated as a diagnostic metric rather than a Core Web Vital.

For AI demonstrations, distinguish:

```text
Page load
↓
Demo initialization
↓
Input ready
↓
Request started
↓
AI processing
↓
Result received
↓
Result rendered
```

Do not report the entire sequence as “page performance.”

---

# 5. Performance Budgets

The project should establish explicit budgets after the existing codebase is audited.

Initial recommended constraints:

### HTML

Keep initial document payload small.

### JavaScript

Avoid loading the entire application and every interactive system on the homepage.

Prefer:

```text
Core shell
+
Page-specific code
+
Interaction-specific code
```

rather than:

```text
Everything
```

### CSS

Avoid large global stylesheets containing unused page-specific rules.

### Images

Optimize every image according to:

- display dimensions
- device density
- format
- compression
- importance above/below the fold

### Fonts

Use a deliberately limited font set.

### Third-party scripts

Every third-party script must justify:

- what it provides
- who receives the data
- performance cost
- whether it is essential

If removing a third-party service improves the site substantially while losing little value, remove it.

---

# 6. Mobile-First Engineering

Mobile is not a secondary rendering target.

For MAKATA.ai, mobile should be treated as a **primary engineering environment** because:

- many visitors will arrive from LinkedIn
- many users will access the site from phones
- the projects themselves involve mobile/Edge AI
- Xylo and Unawain are particularly relevant to mobile computing
- device limitations are part of the site's technical story

### Principle

> **Design for the smallest practical experience first, then expand the information architecture for larger screens.**

Do not build desktop layouts and simply allow them to collapse.

---

# 7. Responsive Composition

Responsive behavior should change the composition of information.

### Desktop

Can support:

```text
Navigation
────────────────────────────────

Project information     System visual
Project information     Architecture
Project information     Evidence
```

### Mobile

Should become:

```text
Project identity

System visual

Primary action

What it does

Architecture

Evidence

Constraints

What changed
```

The information hierarchy remains intact, but the spatial composition changes.

---

# 8. Content-Driven Breakpoints

Do not select breakpoints solely because a framework provides:

```text
sm
md
lg
xl
```

Breakpoints should be determined by when the content stops working.

Test:

- narrow phone
- normal phone
- large phone
- tablet
- small laptop
- standard desktop
- wide desktop

If a two-column architecture breaks at 860px, that is more important than whether 768px is a framework breakpoint.

---

# 9. Mobile Navigation

Desktop navigation:

```text
MAKATA.ai

Work   Lab   Notes   About   Connect
```

Mobile:

```text
MAKATA.ai                         ☰
```

Menu should provide:

- Work
- Lab
- Notes
- About
- Connect

Avoid making navigation itself an elaborate animation.

### Requirements

- keyboard accessible
- screen-reader accessible
- focus trapped appropriately when implemented as a modal menu
- Escape closes the menu
- clear current-page state
- sufficient touch target
- no dependence on hover

---

# 10. Header Behavior

The header should remain useful without consuming excessive mobile screen space.

Possible behavior:

### Desktop

Compact persistent header.

### Mobile

Compact header with optional sticky behavior.

Avoid:

- oversized headers
- permanent promotional banners
- multiple stacked navigation bars
- aggressive hide/show animation
- headers that consume a large percentage of the viewport

The visitor should reach actual engineering content quickly.

---

# 11. Typography

Typography must remain readable before it becomes visually impressive.

Recommended constraints:

- body text approximately 60–80 characters per line on reading layouts
- comfortable paragraph spacing
- adequate heading hierarchy
- no excessively small metadata
- no wall of monospace text
- sufficient contrast

Technical values may use monospace:

```text
MODEL
DEVICE
LATENCY
VERSION
RUNTIME
```

But normal explanatory prose should remain in the primary reading font.

---

# 12. Text Scaling

The interface should remain functional when users increase browser text size.

Do not depend on:

- fixed heights
- clipped headings
- text embedded inside images
- absolute positioning for primary content
- viewport-sized typography that becomes unusable

Test at increased browser text scaling where practical.

---

# 13. Images

Images should be treated as engineering assets rather than decoration.

For each image determine:

1. Is it necessary?
2. Is it above the fold?
3. What dimensions are actually required?
4. Can it be compressed?
5. Can it use a modern format?
6. Can it be lazy loaded?
7. Does it require an accessible alternative?

### Above-the-fold images

Do not lazily load the primary visual if doing so delays the main content experience.

### Below-the-fold images

Lazy-load where appropriate.

### Responsive images

Use appropriate source sizes rather than sending a desktop-sized image to a phone.

---

# 14. Screenshots of AI Systems

Screenshots are useful evidence but can become extremely expensive if used carelessly.

Prefer:

- compressed screenshots
- appropriately sized images
- modern image formats
- responsive variants
- lazy loading

Avoid:

> 4000px screenshot → 390px phone display

unless there is a genuine reason to preserve that resolution.

---

# 15. Video

Video should not automatically autoplay.

For demonstrations:

- poster image first
- lazy load video
- user-controlled playback
- captions where relevant
- avoid unnecessary background video
- respect reduced-motion preferences

A short recorded demonstration can sometimes communicate an interaction more efficiently than loading the entire interactive system.

---

# 16. Fonts

Fonts can create surprisingly large performance costs.

Use only the required weights.

Avoid loading:

```text
100
200
300
400
500
600
700
800
900
```

when the design actually needs:

```text
400
600
```

Consider:

- self-hosting where appropriate
- modern font formats
- `font-display`
- preload only genuinely critical fonts
- avoiding multiple font families unless justified

The site should remain readable if a custom font fails to load.

---

# 17. JavaScript Strategy

JavaScript should be loaded according to user need.

The homepage should not initialize:

- every experiment
- every benchmark visualization
- every architecture explorer
- every demo
- every animation
- every project

Instead:

```text
Initial page
    ↓
Core shell
    ↓
Visible interaction
    ↓
User intent
    ↓
Load additional functionality
```

### Principle

> **Interaction should cause complexity to arrive.**

---

# 18. Code Splitting

Page-specific functionality should be isolated where practical.

For example:

```text
Homepage
├── Core shell
├── Project cards
└── lightweight interactions

Xylo
├── Core shell
├── Demo
├── Architecture
└── benchmark components

Lab
├── Core shell
├── Experiment explorer
└── visualization components
```

Do not force all of these into the initial bundle.

---

# 19. Lazy Loading

Lazy-load components when they are:

- below the fold
- expensive to initialize
- rarely used
- dependent on large libraries
- dependent on AI/API functionality
- interactive only after explicit user action

Examples:

- architecture explorer
- benchmark visualization
- complex graph
- video
- advanced demo
- large code viewer

Do not lazy-load basic content that visitors should see immediately.

---

# 20. Demo Loading Strategy

AI demonstrations are the highest-risk performance surface.

The website must distinguish:

```text
Website is ready.
```

from:

```text
AI system is ready.
```

A visitor should be able to read:

- project description
- architecture
- constraints
- methodology

while the demo initializes independently.

---

# 21. Demo Initialization

Preferred sequence:

```text
Project page loads
        ↓
Demo shell appears
        ↓
Sample input visible
        ↓
Demo capability initialized
        ↓
"Try it" becomes available
```

Avoid:

```text
Page loads
↓
Wait for AI libraries
↓
Wait for model
↓
Wait for API
↓
Finally show page
```

---

# 22. Perceived Performance

Perceived performance matters because AI processing may legitimately take time.

A good demo can make a 5-second computation understandable.

A poor demo can make a 1-second computation feel broken.

During processing, provide factual progress where possible:

```text
READY

Analyzing sample document...

Extracting structure...

Generating response...

Rendering result...
```

Only show stages that actually occur.

Never fabricate progress simply to create the appearance of activity.

---

# 23. Loading States

Every meaningful asynchronous component should have a defined state.

At minimum:

```text
IDLE
READY
PROCESSING
RESULT
ERROR
UNAVAILABLE
```

Where useful:

```text
TIMEOUT
RATE_LIMITED
```

### Loading state requirements

Avoid indefinite spinners.

Prefer:

- skeletons for predictable content
- explicit status for computation
- progress indicators when actual progress exists
- cancellation when technically possible

---

# 24. Skeleton Screens

Use skeletons only when they improve comprehension.

A skeleton should approximate the eventual layout.

Do not use skeletons as decoration.

Avoid:

```text
random gray rectangles
```

that provide no information about what is loading.

For technical demos, explicit state text may be more useful than a skeleton.

---

# 25. Web Workers / Off-Main-Thread Processing

For genuinely CPU-intensive browser-side work, consider:

- Web Workers
- WASM workers
- background computation
- incremental processing

This is especially relevant if MAKATA.ai eventually demonstrates:

- local model inference
- document parsing
- large JSON processing
- graph computation
- benchmark calculations
- visualization transforms

Do not introduce Workers merely because they sound architecturally sophisticated.

Use them when main-thread blocking is measured or strongly expected.

---

# 26. AI Demo Performance

For every live AI demonstration, measure separately:

### Client

- initialization
- serialization
- rendering
- interaction latency

### Network

- request latency
- response latency
- transferred bytes

### AI service

- model queue time
- inference time
- token generation time where relevant

### Total

```text
User action
→ result available
```

These numbers should not automatically be collapsed into one “AI latency” metric.

---

# 27. Precomputed and Hybrid Demos

If live inference is:

- expensive
- unreliable
- slow
- rate limited
- dependent on an external provider

use a hybrid strategy.

Example:

```text
Visitor opens demo
        ↓
Precomputed sample immediately available
        ↓
Visitor chooses "Run live"
        ↓
Live computation
```

This allows the engineering story to remain available even when the live service fails.

---

# 28. Failure Resilience

A demo failure should not become a website failure.

If the API is unavailable:

```text
Demo unavailable

The live system is currently unavailable.

View recorded result →
View architecture →
Read experiment →
```

The project remains explorable.

Never allow an unavailable AI provider to produce:

- blank page
- broken layout
- uncaught exception
- raw server error
- infinite loading state

---

# 29. Caching

Use caching appropriate to the actual hosting architecture.

Potentially cache:

- static HTML
- images
- fonts
- CSS
- JavaScript
- immutable content
- precomputed demo responses

Be cautious with:

- personalized responses
- dynamic AI outputs
- user-submitted documents
- sensitive data

Caching should not accidentally expose one user's dynamic response to another user.

---

# 30. CDN

Static content should preferably be delivered through the hosting platform's CDN or equivalent infrastructure where available.

Prioritize caching for:

- project content
- images
- static assets
- experiment pages
- documentation
- benchmark pages

The objective is not infrastructure complexity.

The objective is:

> **Make immutable content cheap and fast to deliver.**

---

# 31. Prefetching

Prefetch only when there is strong evidence that the visitor is likely to need the resource.

Good candidates:

- project page after intentional interaction with a project card
- next page after an explicit navigation action
- lightweight critical assets

Bad candidates:

- every project
- every demo
- every Lab visualization
- every image on the website

Prefetching can easily become disguised eager loading.

---

# 32. Preloading

Use preload sparingly.

Good candidates may include:

- critical font
- truly critical hero asset

Do not preload everything that might eventually become useful.

A preload is effectively a priority claim on the browser's network.

---

# 33. Layout Stability

Avoid layout shifts caused by:

- images without dimensions
- late-loading fonts
- dynamically inserted banners
- demo components changing height unexpectedly
- architecture diagrams expanding after initialization
- asynchronous content appearing without reserved space

Every major media element should have predictable dimensions or aspect-ratio behavior.

Interactive components should reserve enough space to prevent major shifts.

---

# 34. Architecture Diagram Performance

Architecture diagrams can become surprisingly expensive.

Start with:

```text
HTML/SVG
```

before introducing a large visualization framework.

For static architecture:

- semantic HTML
- lightweight SVG
- CSS

may be sufficient.

Use heavier graph libraries only when the interaction genuinely requires them.

---

# 35. Architecture Diagram Accessibility

Every visual architecture diagram must have a textual representation.

For example:

```text
Architecture

1. User selects a document.
2. Document enters the document-processing layer.
3. Local model analyzes the document.
4. Structured result is returned.
5. Result is rendered in the application.
```

The visual diagram supplements this information.

It must not be the only way to understand the architecture.

---

# 36. Benchmark Accessibility

Charts and benchmark visualizations must not rely solely on visual interpretation.

Provide:

- metric name
- value
- unit
- conditions
- device
- model
- methodology
- date/version where relevant
- textual interpretation

If a chart compares multiple systems, provide a table or accessible equivalent where practical.

---

# 37. Color

Color must never be the only signal.

For example, do not communicate:

```text
green = measured
yellow = observed
red = hypothesis
```

Instead use:

```text
MEASURED
OBSERVED
HYPOTHESIS
```

with color serving only as reinforcement.

This is especially important for:

- status
- benchmark results
- errors
- architecture states
- epistemic labels

---

# 38. Accessibility Standard

Target **WCAG 2.2 AA-oriented accessibility** for the public website.

The implementation should cover at least:

### Structure

- semantic HTML
- meaningful heading hierarchy
- landmarks
- proper lists
- meaningful buttons and links

### Keyboard

- all functionality keyboard accessible
- visible focus
- logical focus order
- no keyboard traps

### Screen readers

- meaningful labels
- accessible names
- status announcements where appropriate
- meaningful link text
- alternative representations for complex visualizations

### Forms

- associated labels
- clear validation
- understandable errors
- instructions where necessary
- accessible success/failure states

### Contrast

Maintain sufficient text and interface contrast.

Do not use low-contrast gray text merely because it looks “technical.”

---

# 39. Focus Management

Interactive components must manage focus intentionally.

Examples:

### Mobile menu

Opening the menu should establish an appropriate focus context.

Closing it should return focus appropriately.

### Dialog

Focus should move into the dialog and not escape unexpectedly.

### Demo result

If the result appears after a user action, the interface should make the change discoverable to assistive technology without unnecessarily stealing focus.

---

# 40. Touch Targets

Interactive controls should be comfortably usable on touchscreens.

Avoid tiny:

- icon buttons
- close buttons
- navigation targets
- benchmark controls
- architecture nodes

The design should not depend on precision tapping.

---

# 41. Hover Independence

No important information should require hover.

If something appears on hover:

- provide an equivalent interaction on touch
- ensure keyboard access
- consider whether the information should simply be visible

This matters particularly for technical metadata and architecture diagrams.

---

# 42. Motion

Motion should communicate:

- state change
- causality
- hierarchy
- interaction
- system behavior

Avoid motion that exists only because an AI website is expected to look “futuristic.”

Avoid:

- particle backgrounds
- perpetual floating objects
- excessive parallax
- constant pulsing
- decorative data streams
- long entrance animations

---

# 43. Reduced Motion

Respect:

```text
prefers-reduced-motion
```

When enabled:

- remove nonessential animation
- reduce transition duration
- disable decorative motion
- preserve functional state changes

The site should remain completely understandable without animation.

---

# 44. Animation Performance

Prefer:

- transform
- opacity

for lightweight transitions.

Avoid expensive layout-triggering animation where unnecessary.

Do not animate:

- large DOM trees
- complex SVG graphs
- huge background surfaces

continuously.

---

# 45. Accessibility of AI Processing

Processing states must be understandable without relying on animation.

Instead of:

> spinning circle

use:

> **Analyzing document…**

For multi-stage processing:

```text
Analyzing document
2 of 4 stages complete
```

only when the stage information is real.

If the system cannot determine progress:

> **Processing… This may take a few seconds.**

is preferable to fabricated percentages.

---

# 46. Accessibility of Errors

Errors should explain:

1. what happened
2. whether the user's input is valid
3. whether retrying may work
4. what alternative is available

Example:

```text
The live demo is temporarily unavailable.

Your document was not uploaded.

You can view a recorded example or try again later.
```

Avoid:

```text
500 INTERNAL SERVER ERROR
```

as the primary user-facing message.

---

# 47. Accessibility of Code

Code blocks should:

- identify language where useful
- provide readable contrast
- remain horizontally scrollable where necessary
- have copy functionality with an accessible label
- not depend exclusively on syntax colors

Do not force long lines to wrap if wrapping destroys code readability.

---

# 48. Accessibility of Tables

Benchmark tables should:

- use semantic table markup
- have clear headers
- associate headers with data
- remain usable on mobile

On small screens, consider:

```text
horizontal scrolling
```

rather than destroying the table structure.

Do not automatically transform every technical table into cards if the transformation makes comparison harder.

---

# 49. Mobile Technical Content

Technical content must remain usable on small screens.

Potential solutions:

- horizontal code scrolling
- horizontally scrollable benchmark tables
- collapsible advanced architecture details
- stacked metadata
- simplified architecture overview
- progressive disclosure

Do not simply shrink desktop technical content until it becomes unreadable.

---

# 50. Progressive Disclosure

Advanced information should be available without overwhelming the initial experience.

Example:

```text
How it works
    ↓
Overview

View technical architecture
    ↓
Detailed architecture

Inspect implementation
    ↓
Repository / technical notes
```

The deeper information remains accessible.

It is simply not required to understand the first layer.

---

# 51. Performance Testing Matrix

Test at minimum across:

### Devices

- older iPhone
- recent iPhone
- Android mid-range device
- desktop/laptop

### Network

- strong Wi-Fi
- ordinary mobile connection
- throttled connection
- high-latency connection

### Browser

- Safari
- Chrome
- at least one additional major browser where relevant

The exact device matrix should be adjusted after observing actual traffic.

---

# 52. Real-Device Testing

Synthetic testing is necessary but insufficient.

At minimum, test:

1. homepage
2. Xylo project page
3. Unawain project page
4. DCS project page
5. Lab
6. one experiment
7. live demo

Evaluate:

- first meaningful content
- scrolling
- navigation
- interaction response
- demo initialization
- demo execution
- visual stability
- thermal/device behavior where relevant

---

# 53. Synthetic Testing

Use tools such as:

- Lighthouse
- PageSpeed Insights
- browser performance tooling
- automated accessibility testing
- bundle analysis

Use these to detect regressions.

Do not optimize exclusively for the score.

A website can achieve an excellent synthetic score while still producing a poor experience on a real device.

---

# 54. Accessibility Testing

Automated testing should be supplemented by manual testing.

### Automated

Check:

- missing labels
- missing alt attributes
- contrast issues
- landmark problems
- heading issues
- invalid ARIA
- keyboard-related structural problems

### Manual

Test:

- keyboard-only navigation
- focus visibility
- screen reader navigation
- zoom/text scaling
- reduced motion
- touch interaction
- error recovery

---

# 55. Bundle Analysis

After the redesign, inspect:

- initial JavaScript
- route-specific bundles
- largest dependencies
- duplicate dependencies
- visualization libraries
- icon libraries
- analytics
- AI/demo packages

For every large dependency ask:

> What visitor-facing capability requires this?

If there is no strong answer, remove it.

---

# 56. Dependency Discipline

Avoid adding dependencies for trivial functionality.

For example, do not install a large library to:

- animate one element
- format one date
- render a simple card
- create a basic tooltip
- create a simple SVG
- perform trivial state management

Every dependency introduces:

- bundle cost
- maintenance
- security surface
- upgrade burden
- possible incompatibilities

---

# 57. Performance Monitoring

After launch, monitor:

### Website

- Core Web Vitals
- page load failures
- JavaScript errors
- route failures

### Demos

- initialization failures
- API latency
- inference latency
- timeout rate
- provider errors
- rate limiting
- completion rate

### Accessibility

- reported issues
- regression testing
- major UI changes

---

# 58. Performance vs Technical Demonstration

A critical distinction:

A technical demo is allowed to be computationally complex.

The **website shell is not**.

For example:

> Xylo demonstrates expensive document processing.

That does not justify:

> MAKATA.ai itself requiring a large model or giant JavaScript bundle merely to display Xylo's description.

The complexity should exist where it demonstrates engineering value.

---

# 59. Mobile AI Demonstrations

For local/on-device demonstrations, explicitly distinguish:

```text
Website running in browser
```

from:

```text
Original application running on device
```

Do not create a browser simulation that visually implies native/on-device execution unless it actually reproduces that architecture.

Where a web demo is only representative, label it accordingly.

---

# 60. Performance Claims

Every performance statement should identify its evidence level.

### Strong

> Measured at 1.8s LCP under [specified test conditions].

### Appropriate

> In our test on [device], inference took approximately 4.2 seconds.

### Appropriate observation

> Consecutive inference caused noticeably longer response times on the tested device.

### Inappropriate

> The app is extremely fast.

unless the claim has a clearly defined and defensible basis.

---

# 61. What Cannot Be Concluded

Performance testing must preserve uncertainty.

A single benchmark cannot establish:

- performance on every device
- performance on every browser
- performance under every network condition
- long-session thermal behavior
- universal AI latency
- universal battery impact
- universal accessibility
- absence of accessibility defects

Likewise, passing an automated accessibility checker does not prove that the site is fully accessible.

The site should therefore preserve:

```text
Measured
Observed
Inferred
Hypothesis
```

in its technical content.

---

# 62. Performance Regression Policy

A change should trigger performance review when it introduces:

- new major dependency
- new visualization library
- new third-party service
- live AI functionality
- large media
- significant JavaScript
- new animation system
- complex client-side state
- large code viewer
- model/runtime logic in the browser

Do not wait until the end of the redesign to discover that the new architecture is slower.

---

# 63. Accessibility Regression Policy

A change should trigger accessibility review when it modifies:

- navigation
- dialogs
- forms
- demo controls
- architecture diagrams
- benchmark visualizations
- custom interactive controls
- keyboard behavior
- focus behavior
- dynamic status messages

---

# 64. Responsive Regression Policy

Test every major redesign change at:

```text
Mobile
Tablet
Desktop
Wide desktop
```

The exact dimensions should come from actual content breakpoints rather than arbitrary framework defaults.

---

